      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I can understand how disheartening this situation is when everything seems to have been tried and it continues to fail. If data preservation isn't a concern and you've reached the point where you've decided that future repair is futile, a complete hardware replacement might indeed be the cleanest solution. However, if you're inclined to see if there's any use left in your current system, attempting a hard shutdown or even a power cycle (unplugging the system, waiting a bit, and then plugging it back in) could occasionally resolve issues stemming from stuck processes or minor UPS-related settings. But if a shutdown is still not possible due to the crashes, unfortunately, the last reported symptoms suggest hardware failure.\n\nIt might be helpful to consult with a professional technician who could diagnose the exact cause of the crashing and provide a definitive verdict on whether the motherboard or another component is at fault. A new system might indeed offer you peace and performance you are seeking, and while costly, could be a worthwhile investment in the long term.\n\nPlease remember that enduring service from ill-functioning hardware can be stressful and costly, but ensuring a reliable computing environment requires rebuilding the fundamental components affecting performance. If an upgrade has been on the horizon and the machine in question is near the end of its usable life",
